/**
\defgroup basis_fourier Fourier bases
\brief Complete periodic Fourier trigonometric basis with analytical derivatives to order 6, integrals from the lower boundary, and closed-form cross Gram matrices.

Include `<laplace/basis/fourier.hpp>` to use \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis "fourier_basis", constructed on the host by \ref laplace::basis::make_fourier_basis "make_fourier_basis()". Point evaluation functions marked `LAPLACE_HD` run on the host and inside Kokkos kernels on the device, whereas cross Gram matrix generation and semi-bandwidth queries run on the host. Across all tested configurations, row errors stay within 2.5 times the angle conditioning, integrals within 2.0 ulp, and cross Gram matrices within 18 ulp.

## Mathematical definition

Let \f$x_{\mathrm{lo}}\f$ (`lower`) and \f$x_{\mathrm{hi}}\f$ (`upper`) be the domain boundaries with \f$x_{\mathrm{lo}} < x_{\mathrm{hi}}\f$, \f$\omega > 0\f$ the fundamental period, and \f$H \ge 0\f$ the integer number of harmonics. The basis has dimension \f$K = 2H + 1\f$. With shifted coordinate \f$x' = x - x_{\mathrm{lo}}\f$ and fundamental angular frequency \f$\nu = 2\pi / \omega\f$, the basis columns \f$b_0(x), \dots, b_{K-1}(x)\f$ are ordered as \f$b_0(x) = 1\f$ and, for each harmonic \f$j = 1, \dots, H\f$,
\f[
b_{2j-1}(x) = \sin(j \nu x'), \quad b_{2j}(x) = \cos(j \nu x').
\f]
The default upper boundary is \f$x_{\mathrm{hi}} = x_{\mathrm{lo}} + \omega\f$, corresponding to one full period. Any \f$x_{\mathrm{hi}} > x_{\mathrm{lo}}\f$ is permitted, and `full_period` records whether \f$x_{\mathrm{hi}} - x_{\mathrm{lo}} = \omega\f$ or \f$x_{\mathrm{hi}} = x_{\mathrm{lo}} + \omega\f$ in floating point. Rows are dense: \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::row_width "row_width()" returns \f$K\f$, the first column index is always \f$0\f$, and \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::scratch_size "scratch_size()" is \f$0\f$.

Derivatives of order \f$k \in \{0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\}\f$ satisfy the trigonometric shift identity
\f[
\frac{\mathrm{d}^k}{\mathrm{d}x^k} \sin(j \nu x') = (j \nu)^k \sin\left(j \nu x' + k \frac{\pi}{2}\right), \quad
\frac{\mathrm{d}^k}{\mathrm{d}x^k} \cos(j \nu x') = (j \nu)^k \cos\left(j \nu x' + k \frac{\pi}{2}\right).
\f]
Modulo 4, rotation maps \f$(\sin, \cos)\f$ to \f$(\sin, \cos)\f$, \f$(\cos, -\sin)\f$, \f$(-\sin, -\cos)\f$, and \f$(-\cos, \sin)\f$ for \f$k \equiv 0, 1, 2, 3\f$, each scaled by \f$(j \nu)^k\f$. Column \f$b_0(x)\f$ is \f$1\f$ at \f$k = 0\f$ and \f$0\f$ for \f$k \ge 1\f$. Given coefficients \f$\beta_0, \dots, \beta_{K-1}\f$, expansions \f$\sum_{i=0}^{K-1} \beta_i b_i^{(k)}(x)\f$ accumulate on the fly without a \f$K\f$-buffer.

Integrals from \f$x_{\mathrm{lo}}\f$ to \f$x\f$ have closed forms. For \f$b_0(t) = 1\f$, \f$\int_{x_{\mathrm{lo}}}^x b_0(t) \, \mathrm{d}t = x - x_{\mathrm{lo}} = x'\f$, and for each harmonic \f$j = 1, \dots, H\f$,
\f[
\int_{x_{\mathrm{lo}}}^x \sin(j \nu (t - x_{\mathrm{lo}})) \, \mathrm{d}t = \frac{1 - \cos(j \nu x')}{j \nu} = \frac{2 \sin^2(j \nu x' / 2)}{j \nu},
\f]
\f[
\int_{x_{\mathrm{lo}}}^x \cos(j \nu (t - x_{\mathrm{lo}})) \, \mathrm{d}t = \frac{\sin(j \nu x')}{j \nu} = \frac{2 \sin(j \nu x' / 2) \cos(j \nu x' / 2)}{j \nu}.
\f]

The cross Gram matrix between derivative orders \f$k\f$ and \f$l\f$ (\f$0 \le k, l \le 6\f$) on \f$[x_{\mathrm{lo}}, x_{\mathrm{hi}}]\f$ is
\f[
G_{kl} = \int_{x_{\mathrm{lo}}}^{x_{\mathrm{hi}}} b^{(k)}(x) (b^{(l)}(x))^\top \mathrm{d}x.
\f]
It is written into a dense \f$K \times K\f$ array in column-major order, so `out[i + j * K]` holds \f$\int_{x_{\mathrm{lo}}}^{x_{\mathrm{hi}}} b_i^{(k)}(x) b_j^{(l)}(x) \, \mathrm{d}x\f$. When `full_period` is true, \f$G_{kl}\f$ is block diagonal with \f$2 \times 2\f$ harmonic blocks of amplitudes \f$A_k = (j \nu)^k\f$ and \f$A_l = (j \nu)^l\f$:
\f[
\begin{pmatrix}
\int_{x_{\mathrm{lo}}}^{x_{\mathrm{hi}}} b_{2j-1}^{(k)} b_{2j-1}^{(l)} \, \mathrm{d}x & \int_{x_{\mathrm{lo}}}^{x_{\mathrm{hi}}} b_{2j-1}^{(k)} b_{2j}^{(l)} \, \mathrm{d}x \\
\int_{x_{\mathrm{lo}}}^{x_{\mathrm{hi}}} b_{2j}^{(k)} b_{2j-1}^{(l)} \, \mathrm{d}x & \int_{x_{\mathrm{lo}}}^{x_{\mathrm{hi}}} b_{2j}^{(k)} b_{2j}^{(l)} \, \mathrm{d}x
\end{pmatrix}
= \frac{1}{2} A_k A_l \omega
\begin{pmatrix}
\cos((k - l) \pi / 2) & \sin((k - l) \pi / 2) \\
-\sin((k - l) \pi / 2) & \cos((k - l) \pi / 2)
\end{pmatrix}.
\f]
For \f$k = l = 0\f$ on a full period, \f$G_{00}\f$ is diagonal with entry \f$(0, 0)\f$ equal to \f$\omega\f$ and remaining diagonal entries equal to \f$\omega / 2\f$. Accordingly, \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::gram_bandwidth "gram_bandwidth()" returns \f$\min(1, 2H)\f$ on a full period and \f$2H\f$ otherwise.

## Domain and special values

The Fourier basis is complete and periodic on \f$\mathbb{R}\f$: it is defined for every real \f$x\f$ and has no outside-domain rule (see \ref basis).

Both \ref laplace::basis::make_fourier_basis "make_fourier_basis()" overloads throw `std::invalid_argument` if `lower`, `upper`, or `period` is not finite, if \f$\omega \le 0\f$, if \f$H < 0\f$, or if \f$x_{\mathrm{lo}} \ge x_{\mathrm{hi}}\f$. Likewise, \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::cross_gram "cross_gram()" throws `std::invalid_argument` if \f$k < 0\f$, \f$k > 6\f$, \f$l < 0\f$, or \f$l > 6\f$.

If \f$x\f$ is NaN, `row()` writes `1.0` to `out[0]` and NaN to remaining entries, `row_d1()` through `row_d6()` write `0.0` to `out[0]` and NaN to remaining entries, and `integral_row()` writes NaN to `out[0]`. Both `integral_row()` and `integral_expansion()` evaluate to \f$0\f$ at \f$x = x_{\mathrm{lo}}\f$. Expansions return NaN when \f$x\f$ or any coefficient \f$\beta_i\f$ is NaN, and `integral_expansion()` returns NaN when \f$x\f$ is NaN.

## Numerical methods

Harmonic pairs \f$(\sin(j \theta), \cos(j \theta))\f$ with \f$\theta = \nu x'\f$ are evaluated using the turn coordinate \f$u = x' / \omega\f$. At anchor harmonics \f$j = 1, 9, 17, \dots\f$, the exact reduction route forms \f$j u\f$ as an exact double-double product using `Kokkos::fma` on the device and Dekker's split on the host. It reduces \f$r = j u - \operatorname{round}(j u)\f$ by adding and subtracting \f$1.5 \times 2^{52}\f$, then evaluates \f$\sin(2\pi r)\f$ and \f$\cos(2\pi r)\f$. Intermediate harmonics advance by planar rotation:
\f[
\cos((j+1)\theta) = \cos(j\theta)\cos\theta - \sin(j\theta)\sin\theta, \quad
\sin((j+1)\theta) = \sin(j\theta)\cos\theta + \cos(j\theta)\sin\theta.
\f]
Re-anchoring every 8 harmonics prevents drift and keeps the error within a few times the angle conditioning \f$2\pi j |x'| / \omega \varepsilon\f$.

Column integrals are computed in a single pass along the recurrence chain over the half angle \f$\phi = \pi u\f$ using \f$2 \sin^2(j\phi)\f$ and \f$2 \sin(j\phi) \cos(j\phi)\f$. The expression \f$1 - \cos(j \nu x')\f$ is never formed, avoiding catastrophic cancellation near zero or integer multiples of \f$2\pi\f$.

Cross Gram matrices use product-to-sum trigonometric reduction. With \f$\tau = x - x_{\mathrm{lo}}\f$ and \f$L = x_{\mathrm{hi}} - x_{\mathrm{lo}}\f$, each derivative column is written as \f$A \sin(j \nu \tau + m \pi / 2)\f$ with amplitude \f$A = (j \nu)^q\f$ and integer phase index \f$m\f$. Applying \f$\sin P \sin Q = \frac{1}{2} (\cos(P - Q) - \cos(P + Q))\f$ reduces matrix entries to integrals over \f$[0, L]\f$: for integer \f$n > 0\f$,
\f[
\int_0^L \cos(n \nu \tau) \, \mathrm{d}\tau = \frac{\sin(n \nu L)}{n \nu}, \quad
\int_0^L \sin(n \nu \tau) \, \mathrm{d}\tau = \frac{2 \sin^2(n \nu L / 2)}{n \nu},
\f]
while for \f$n = 0\f$ the cosine and sine integrals are \f$L\f$ and \f$0\f$. When \f$L = \omega\f$, \f$n \nu L = 2\pi n\f$ makes all \f$n \ge 1\f$ integrals vanish identically, yielding the block-diagonal structure.

## Measured accuracy

See \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured" for error metric definitions, reference generation, and test bound conventions.

Reference values in `tools/reference/basis_fourier.py` use mpmath at 300 bits of precision across 27 configurations and harmonic counts \f$H \in \{0, 1, 4, 20, 100\}\f$. Full-period domains are \f$[0, 1]\f$, \f$[-3, 4.5]\f$ with \f$\omega = 7.5\f$, and \f$[1000, 1001]\f$ with \f$\omega = 1\f$; non-full-period domains are \f$[0, 10]\f$ with \f$\omega = 7\f$, \f$[0, 9]\f$ with \f$\omega = 12\f$, and \f$[1000, 1003]\f$ with \f$\omega = 0.7\f$. Evaluation points include random points, points outside the domain, and points where \f$j u\f$ is near an integer or half-integer. Reference Gram matrices are computed independently by exact integration of complex exponentials.

Row and integral errors are measured in ulp of \f$\max(1, \max_i |b_i^{(k)}(x)|)\f$, and Gram errors in ulp of the largest matrix entry. For derivative order \f$k\f$, the conditioning scale of column \f$j\f$ at \f$x'\f$ is \f$\max(1, 2\pi j |x'| / \omega) + k\f$. Row errors reflect the rounding of \f$x' / \omega\f$ scaled by this angle conditioning, peaking on the shifted domain with \f$x_{\mathrm{lo}} = 1000\f$, \f$\omega = 0.7\f$, and \f$x' \le 3\f$. The regression suite (`tests/basis/test_fourier.cpp`) enforces bounds set to about 1.5 times the measured maxima on gcc/OpenMP and CUDA (RTX 4070 SUPER):

| Harmonics \f$H\f$ | Row, gcc / CUDA (ulp) | Row bound (ulp) | Ratio, gcc / CUDA | Ratio bound | Integral, gcc / CUDA (ulp) | Integral bound (ulp) | Gram (ulp) | Gram bound (ulp) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | — | 0.5 | — | 3.75 | — | 3.0 | — | 27.0 |
| 1 | 24.5 / 24.5 | 37.0 | 1.0 / 1.0 | 3.75 | 2.0 / 1.1 | 3.0 | 11 | 27.0 |
| 4 | 93 / 93 | 140.0 | 1.2 / 1.1 | 3.75 | 2.0 / 2.0 | 3.0 | 11 | 27.0 |
| 20 | 437 / 437 | 660.0 | 2.0 / 2.5 | 3.75 | 2.0 / 2.0 | 3.0 | 18 | 27.0 |
| 100 | 2147 / 2147 | 3300.0 | 2.0 / 2.0 | 3.75 | 1.5 / 1.2 | 3.0 | 11 | 27.0 |

See `docs/numerics.md` for candidate route comparisons.

## Timings

Evaluating the sine and cosine pairs for \f$H = 100\f$ with re-anchored rotation takes 633 ns per row on one gcc thread and 5.3 ns on CUDA (RTX 4070 SUPER).

## Example

The following example constructs a Fourier basis with 4 harmonics on \f$[0, 365.25]\f$ and evaluates rows inside a `Kokkos::parallel_for` kernel:

\code{.cpp}
#include <laplace/basis/fourier.hpp>
#include <Kokkos_Core.hpp>

void eval_fourier_kernel(const Kokkos::View<const double*>& x,
                         Kokkos::View<double**, Kokkos::LayoutRight>& out,
                         int n) {
  constexpr int H = 4;
  constexpr int K = 2 * H + 1;
  constexpr double lower = 0.0;
  constexpr double period = 365.25;

  const auto basis = laplace::basis::make_fourier_basis(lower, period, H);

  Kokkos::parallel_for("eval_fourier", n, KOKKOS_LAMBDA(const int i) {
    double row_values[K];
    basis.row(x(i), row_values);
    for (int col = 0; col < K; ++col) {
      out(i, col) = row_values[col];
    }
  });
}
\endcode

## Module functions and types

- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis "fourier_basis" — Lightweight Fourier basis structure
- \ref laplace::basis::make_fourier_basis(double, double, int, double) "make_fourier_basis(lower, period, harmonics, upper)" — Constructs a Fourier basis on a custom domain
- \ref laplace::basis::make_fourier_basis(double, double, int) "make_fourier_basis(lower, period, harmonics)" — Constructs a Fourier basis on one full period

### Member functions of the Fourier basis

- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::dimension "dimension()" — Total number of basis functions \f$K = 2H + 1\f$
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::row_width "row_width()" — Number of columns in each evaluated row (\f$K\f$)
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::scratch_size "scratch_size()" — Scratch buffer size needed for evaluation (0)
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::gram_bandwidth "gram_bandwidth()" — Semi-bandwidth of cross Gram matrices
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::row "row()" — Evaluates basis row at order 0
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::row_d1 "row_d1()" — Evaluates basis row at derivative order 1
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::row_d2 "row_d2()" — Evaluates basis row at derivative order 2
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::row_d3 "row_d3()" — Evaluates basis row at derivative order 3
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::row_d4 "row_d4()" — Evaluates basis row at derivative order 4
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::row_d5 "row_d5()" — Evaluates basis row at derivative order 5
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::row_d6 "row_d6()" — Evaluates basis row at derivative order 6
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::expansion "expansion()" — Evaluates linear expansion \f$\sum \beta_i b_i(x)\f$
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::expansion_d1 "expansion_d1()" — Evaluates expansion at derivative order 1
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::expansion_d2 "expansion_d2()" — Evaluates expansion at derivative order 2
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::expansion_d3 "expansion_d3()" — Evaluates expansion at derivative order 3
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::expansion_d4 "expansion_d4()" — Evaluates expansion at derivative order 4
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::expansion_d5 "expansion_d5()" — Evaluates expansion at derivative order 5
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::expansion_d6 "expansion_d6()" — Evaluates expansion at derivative order 6
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::integral_row "integral_row()" — Evaluates column integrals from lower boundary to \f$x\f$
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::integral_expansion "integral_expansion()" — Evaluates integrated expansion from lower boundary to \f$x\f$
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::cross_gram "cross_gram()" — Evaluates cross Gram matrix \f$G_{kl}\f$ in closed form

### Data members of the Fourier basis

- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::lower "lower" — Lower bound of the domain
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::upper "upper" — Upper bound of the domain
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::period "period" — Fundamental period \f$\omega > 0\f$
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::harmonics "harmonics" — Number of harmonics \f$H \ge 0\f$
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::full_period "full_period" — Whether the domain is exactly one full period
*/
